<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=friction-match">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friction match</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lucifer, Match</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A friction match is a small stick tipped with a mixture that ignites when struck against a rough surface. Pinewood sticks tipped with sulfur, lit from a flame, are described in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> around 950, and in 1805 Jean Chancel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> made a match lit by dipping it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulfuric-acid">sulfuric acid</a>. The first commercially successful friction match was made by John Walker, a chemist and pharmacist of Stockton-on-Tees, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and first recorded in April 1827: sticks tipped with antimony trisulfide and potassium chlorate. Samuel Jones sold an improved version as the lucifer, and Gustaf Erik Pasch’s striking surface of red phosphorus of 1844 led to the safety match.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friction-match.jpg" alt="Friction match" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastian Ritter (Rise0011) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Streichholz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1827</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Stockton-on-Tees, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>John Walker · Isaac Holden · Charles Sauria</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Walker may have made his first friction matches during experiments in 1826. Earlier French friction lighters, Cagniard de la Tour’s phosphorus bottle of 1810 and François Derosne’s briquet phosphorique of 1816, proved inconvenient and unsafe.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/control-of-fire">Control of fire<span>c. 1 million years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=friction-match">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
